  "language_probability": 1.0,
  "text": "Cut a gold coin in half, then cut it again and again. How small can you go before it stops being gold? What's really inside an atom? By the end of this film, you'll know why the periodic table is in the order it is and how we proved it. Because in 1869, the table had a secret problem. It was sorted by weight, and some elements refused to fit. If weight was the rule, the rule was broken, and nobody knew why. For 2,000 years, the atom was just an idea, the smallest piece, the one you can't cut. Nobody had ever seen one. Then, in a glowing glass tube, someone found a piece smaller than the atom. Then a tiny bullet bounced back off a sheet of gold as if it had hit a wall. And the final proof came from a young physicist with a beam of X-rays who counted what was inside every element. Four people, one century, and a question that started with a knife and a coin. So why was the table in the wrong order?",
